00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.720
Hello, everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel
and my name is Paul.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.680
Today, we'll be answering the question...

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.280
How similar are Spanish and Italian?

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.740
Spanish and Italian are both members
 of the Romance Language Family,

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.360
which developed from dialects of Vulgar Latin 
which were spoken throughout the Roman Empire.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.780
For more information on their history and development,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.140
check out my video on the 
Romance Language Family right here

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.360
Like any pair of Romance languages,
Spanish and italian have a lot in common,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.900
and they are mutually intelligible to some extent,

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.840
especially in their written form 
and when spoken slowly and clearly.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.500
Some speakers of Spanish and Italian report 
that they are able to have extended conversations 
with speakers of the other language,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.800
provided that they both speak slowly and clearly 
and they stop to clarify unfamiliar vocabulary.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.100
But, despite their similarity, Spanish and
Italian are also different in many ways.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.860
Let's examine both of the languages together and 
we'll see just what's similar and different about them.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:03.960
 

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.900
Spanish and Italian have about 82% lexical similarity

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.680
That means that 82% of the words in either language 
will have cognate vocabulary in the other language.

