These sentences mean "I need to find a bank".

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.980
In these sentences, we see "need" expressed 
in different ways than the last pair of sentences.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.440
In Spanish, its "tengo que" while, in Italian, it's "devo".

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.640
But, in Spanish, it's also possible to say "debo",
 an obvious cognate of "devo".

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.040
The word meaning "to find" is also different.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.940
In Spanish, it's "encontrar" but in Italian, it's "trovare".

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.500
Actually the Spanish word can also mean "to meet".

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.840
And there's a different italian word "incontrare"
which is a cognate of "encontrar".

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.020
But it only means "to meet", not "to find".

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.040
Next, you can see that the words for "bank" are cognates.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.240
But, interestingly, they have different gender.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.660
In Spanish, it's masculine which we can see from 
the article "un" and the "o" at the end of "banco"

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.060
And in Italian, it's feminine which we can see from 
the article "una" and the "a" at the end of "banca".

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:01.340
So, from these examples, we can see that there's a lot of
crossover between Spanish vocabulary and Italian vocabulary.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.520
But there are also some "false friends" to watch out for.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:05.760
False friends?

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.220
No, I don't mean two faced people 
who talk behind your back.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.960
I mean "words that look and sound similar in either 
language and likely share a common origin,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.880
but they have a different meaning 
or usage in each language. For example:

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.780
In Spanish, "pronto" means "soon" 
In Italian, "pronto"  means "ready".

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.580
In Spanish, "guardar" means "to keep" or "to guard".
In Italian "guadare" means "to look at".

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.220
In Spanish, "oficina" means "office".
In Italian,  "officina" means "workshop".

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.700
There's a different word in Italian "ufficio"
which means "office".

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:45.880
In Spanish, "embarazada" means "pregnant" 
but, in Italian, "imbarazzata" means "embarrassed"

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:51.840
False friends like these are a common source 
of confusion for people who learn a language 
that's similar to one they already know.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.420
If we only look at lexical similarity, Spanish has more 
in common with Portuguese than it does with Italian.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.760
And Italian has more in common
with French than it does with Spanish.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.040
In either case, it's 89% lexical similarity.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:08.580
But, Spanish and Italian are more similar and
pronunciation than those other pairs of languages,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.260
which can sometimes lead to more intelligibility.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.280
 

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.280
Spanish has five vowel sounds.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.020
Italian also has these five vowel sounds,
plus two additional ones.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:25.000
Italian has 2 "e" sounds open e : "..", like the "e" in "bed"

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.900
and closed e : "..." like the vowel sound in "say"

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.180
It also has 2 "o" sounds. 
Open "o" ... like the "o" in "pop".

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.760
And a closed o sound ... like the "o" in "Pope".

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.500
And both Spanish and Italian 
have a similar rhotic R sound.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.360
One notable difference between Spanish
and Italian pronunciation is that

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.480
almost all Italian words end in a vowel,
but in Spanish, this is not the case.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.580
For example. In Italian "costare" which means "to cost",
but, in Spanish, it's "costar".

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.620
So the Italian word ends in a vowel 
while the Spanish word ends in a consonant.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.020
If you watch out for this, you'll notice it quite a lot.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:03.760
 

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.420
In both Spanish and Italian "c" and "g"
become soft when followed by "i" or "e".

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.900
But the soft sound is different in each language.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:17.740
In Spanish, the soft "c" sounds like "th" or "s".
In Italian, it sounds like "tch".

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.000
In Spanish, soft "g" sounds like "..." 
In Italian, it sounds like "j"

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.940
For example in Spanish:

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:27.280
and in Italian:

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.540
This means "city".

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:30.580
In Spanish:

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:32.800
means "people"

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.180
but, in Italian, it's "gente"

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:36.600
In Spanish:

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.580
