In Italian, it's "...."

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.060
Some words like those above 
are cognates that sound different.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.700
There are other words that sound more similar 
but have to be written differently to reflect their sound.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:56.500
The word for "what" in Spanish is "que", spelled with "qu"
and, in Italian, it's "che" spelt with "ch"

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.780
In Spanish, if you want to have the hard "c" sound 
before "i" or "e", you have to spell it as "qu".

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.280
In Italian, you have to spell it as "ch"

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:11.180
And while "ch" always represents a hard "c" sound in Italian, 
in Spanish, it represents the sound "tch" like in English

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:18.700
So, in Italian, you have cioccolato, spelled with "ci" 
at the beginning but, in Spanish, 
you have "chocolate" a spelled with "ch".

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.680
In Spanish, you have "cuando" meaning "when" 
and "cuanto" meaning "how much".

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.580
And in Italian you have "quando" and "quanto"

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:31.420
In Spanish, you can't write it with "qu" because 
that always represents a hard "c" sound.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:34.660
So the words would end up sounding like "..." and "..."

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.280
Some of the differences in pronunciation and 
orthography between Spanish and Italian are fairly systematic.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:45.320
For example, e in Italian often becomes "ie" in Spanish. 
For example:

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:46.660
becomes

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:49.160
becomes

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.780
And, when, in Italian, you have "sc", "sf" or "sp" or "st"
at the beginning of a word,

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.500
in Spanish, these are often preceded by an "e".

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.700
For example, "scrivere" in Italian 
becomes "escribir" in Spanish.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.300
And "speciale" in Italian becomes "especial" in Spanish.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:13.120
One other thing to note is that double
consonants are very common in Italian.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.660
But, in Spanish, most consonants can't be doubled.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.700
For example, in Italian, "gatto" means "cat" 
but, in Spanish, its "gato".

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.560
And, in Italian, "tazza" out means "cup" 
but it's "taza" in Spanish.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:26.840
 

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.960
The general word order of Spanish and Italian 
is very similar. Have a look at these two sentences.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:32.920
In Spanish:

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.080
