00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:09.220
In this case, the auxiliary verb in both languages.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:13.140
Italian grammar has a couple of complications 
that Spanish doesn't have.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.120
 

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.060
Articles in Italian are more complicated than it Spanish.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:23.120
In Spanish, the indefinite articles are "un"
for masculine nouns and "una" for feminine nouns.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.500
Also, in Italian, it's "un" and "una".

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:30.980
But "un" becomes "uno" before words beginning 
in S or Z followed by another consonant.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:35.020
and "una" become "un' " 
before a noun beginning with a vowel.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.880
So, in Italian, we have "un passaporto" but "uno studente".

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:42.140
And we have "una chiave" but "un' italiana".

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.780
Spanish and Italian both have four basic definite articles.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.800
In Spanish, they are "el"  for masculine singular, 
"la" for feminine singular,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.200
"los" for masculine plural, "las" for feminine plural.
And that is it !

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:00.900
In Italian, they're "il" for masculine singular,
"la" for feminine singular

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.580
"i" for masculine plural and "le" for  feminine plural.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:09.320
But "il" becomes "lo" before words beginning
with S or Z plus another consonnant.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:12.200
and it becomes " l' " before a vowel.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:16.320
and "i" becomes "gli" before S or Z 
plus another consonnant.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:21.660
So we have "il passaporto" but "lo studente"
which are both masculine singular.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:26.900
And we have "i passaporti" but "gli studenti" 
which are both masculine plural.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.780
Also both "la" and "le"  become " l ' " before a vowel.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:33.880
 

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.060
The way possessive pronouns 
are used is also a little bit different.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:41.120
In Italian, possessive pronouns are normally
used together with a definite article.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.020
Except when we talk about family members.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.780
But, in Spanish, the definite article is not required.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:51.080
So, in Italian, you have "la mia bicicletta"
and in Spanish, you have "mi bicicleta"

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.220
So notice in Spanish, there's no definite article "la".

00:10:54.300 --> 00:11:00.240
Another difference to notice here is that, in Spanish, 
there is no distinction between 
masculine and feminine possessive pronouns.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:04.860
In Italian, there's "mia" for feminine singular 
and "mio" for masculine singular.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:07.500
But, in Spanish, there's just "mi" for both.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:08.760
 

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:13.700
The verb tenses use and the way that verbs are 
conjugated is really quite similar in both languages.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:18.140
Of course, the exact conjugations are not
quite the same but they are fairly similar.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.560
Here's a sentence meaning "They will live in New York".

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:25.180
The verb to live in Italian is "vivere" 
and, in Spanish, it's "vivir"

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:26.820
The sentence in Italian :

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:29.480
in Spanish:

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.200
We can see that the third person plural
future tense conjugation is similar

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:39.060
but notice that the Italian one ends 
in a double consonant and a vowel.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:43.340
Now let's look at a sentence in the past.
In the present perfect to be precise.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.800
This sentence means 
"She bought too many things".

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:46.600
In Italian :

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:49.740
and in Spanish

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.800
The present perfect tense is formed 
the same way in both languages.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.680
First here is the auxiliary verb meaning "to have".

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.040
"avere" in Italian, "haber" in Spanish

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.700
That's followed by the past participle 
of the verb meaning "to buy".

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.140
"comprare" in Italian and "comprar" in Spanish.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:17.320
One thing to note about the present perfect tense is that, 
in Italian, most verbs take the auxiliary verb "avere" "to have".

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.360
But some verbs take "essere" meaning "to be"
as the auxiliary verb.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:26.920
That's similar to in French but, in Spanish,
 the only auxiliary verb is "haber", meaning "to have".

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:33.060
So, in Italian, there's "è entrato" meaning "he entered" 
but, in Spanish, it's "a entrado"

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.800
Those are just a couple of examples but, basically,

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:40.660
all verb tenses and all verb forms have 
an equivalent or a parallel in the other language.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:41.860
 

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.700
Let's just look at a couple more sentences
and see what we find along the way.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:49.320
This sentence means :
"Maria is less intelligent than her sister"

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.840
In Spanish:

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:57.320
And in Italian:

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:02.720
Here we can see that the word order is the same and
we can basically translate the sentences word for word.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:07.380
The third person female form of the verb
"to be" is similar but slightly different.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.380
The word for less is similar but slightly different.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:15.840
The word for intelligent is similar but, in Spanish,
the G sound is softened to a "...",

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:20.360
while, in Italian, it's softened to a "..."
and the Italian word has a double l.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.420
The preposition used is also different.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.640
The possessive pronoun is similar but, 
in Spanish, there is no gender distinction.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.320
And the word for "sister" is different in the two languages.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:35.780
As you can see Spanish and Italian have a lot 
of similarities including basically the same word order,

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:41.160
similar grammar, a lot of similar vocabulary 
and generally speaking similar pronunciation.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:46.380
But there are still significant differences, particularly 
in vocabulary, pronunciation and orthography.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.160
They are different enough that they are,
unmistakeably, two different languages

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:54.240
but they're similar enough that there is 
a certain degree of mutual intelligibility.

00:13:54.320 --> 00:14:00.840
And that degree of mutual intelligibility can 
probably be greatly increased by studying 
even the basics of the other language.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:04.320
But, producing the other language accurately 
is  obviously another matter

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.120
and that would require dedicated study.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.720
Okay. The question of the day for 
native speakers of Spanish and Italian:

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.360
How well can you understand the other language, 
both in speech and in writing?

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:18.540
And have you ever tried to converse with a speaker 
of the other language using only your native languages?

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:19.800
How did that work out?

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.860
And for learners of Spanish and Italian:

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:25.780
How much does your knowledge of one of 
those languages help you make sense of the other?

